+
+The fixes matter: the literal fixed-field notation on p.201 is defective under
+Moh's p.141 definition; the p.171 simplified ODE display reverses its weights;
+the intermediate prefix in Prop.5.3 is not generally the next canonical centre;
+and matching Moh's table does not refute a weaker necessary screen. The frozen
+report's ancillary “L=1 always” count also needs a precise restriction. None of
+these fixes changes 90. No new exit-price assertion is made.
+
+**1. Custody and scope.** Before mathematical reads, I joined the nine numbered
+`charged_input_<i>_basename` and `_sha256` receipt fields with `awk`, wrote
+`box/actual-stabilizer-screen-gate-20260906/inputs.sha256`, and ran `sha256sum -c`:
+**9/9 OK**, no mismatch. All charged reads used `/tmp/jc2-lane.rMB50I/inputs`.
+The receipt was `xmodel/actual-stabilizer-screen-gate-astra-20260906.run.v2`.
+No sub-agents, ledger edits, jc2-lean, or ideation inputs were used. No uncharged
+operative implementation was read or executed. The frozen skeleton was executed
+only as a comparison after my own census and screens had been computed.
+
+Moh's required pp.170–171, 179–180, 187–190, 200–203 and 207 were inspected as
+page images, rendered from the frozen PDF; printed page = PDF ordinal + 139.
+I additionally inspected pp.141, 185, 205–206 to resolve notation, the top window,
+and the ODE. Renderings and OCR used for navigation are temporary files outside
+the deliverables. The mathematical sources below are printed propositions and
+displays, not previous validators' verdicts.
+
+The pair in the theorem is a non-coordinate unit-Jacobian pair, minimal in degree
+sum, in Moh's normalized FS presentation: `deg g=deg_y g=n`, `deg f=m<n`,
+`M_1=-m`, `M_s=n-2`, `delta_s=-1`, and a tower of major discs. Minimality is
+essential to exclusions by degree reduction. This gate does not extend those
+exclusions to arbitrary nonminimal presentations or to descended pairs with
+Jacobian `x^ell`.
+
+**2. What the field sentence actually says.** Equation (8), p.201, explicitly
+defines the coarse integer `L=lcm(den delta_s,...,den delta_r)` and
+`A_{r-1}=den(L delta_{r-1})`. Its following automorphism sentence motivates
+(10)/(11). Def.5.1(4), p.179, contains no L: it specifies the unique general point
+`sigma_i=c_i+pi t^{delta_i}` and invokes Prop.4.6. Its sum of coefficients is not
+a declaration that all indices are integral.
+
+There is a literal notation defect overlooked by the frozen report. On p.141,
+`k<<t>>` is defined as the entire Puiseux field `union_N k((t^{1/N}))`.
+Consequently `k<<bar t>>=k<<bar t^A>>`; a nonidentity automorphism of the former
+**over the latter** is impossible. The report's asserted equality
+`k<<bar t^A>>=k((t^{1/L}))` is false with Moh's definition.
+
+The valid construction is the finite Laurent extension
+`k((bar t))/k((bar t^A))`, where `bar t^{LA}=t`, or an extension of this action
+to the Puiseux field fixing `K_L:=k((t^{1/L}))`. The Laurent interpretation is
+also the one written with ordinary parentheses in p.188's conjugation argument.
+If `c_j in K_L` and `A=den(L delta_j)`, then `a=LA delta_j` is integral,
+`gcd(a,A)=1`, and `bar t -> omega bar t` sends
+`sigma_j(pi)` to `sigma_j(omega^a pi)`. It fixes t and every coefficient of
+`g,T_i^psi`, since these are in `k[t^{-1}][y]`.
+
+Thus the mathematical role of L in the action is to supply a field containing
+the centre. It also defines the A used algebraically in (9)–(13); “only names a
+field” should not obscure that dependence. There is no separate requirement in
+Def.5.1(4) that this field contain every earlier radius monomial with a zero
+coefficient. The bottom argument works at the smaller centre field by the direct
+proof in paragraph 5. The sharpening is a proved strengthening of (8), not its
+literal definition and not a claim about which program Moh ran.
+
+**3. Independent Lemma A: exact denominator lattice.** Write `c_i` for the
+canonical common truncation strictly below `delta_i` of roots in `D_i`, and put
+
+```
+Lcal_i = lcm{den(delta_j): i<j<=s and C_j != 0},     lcm(empty)=1.
+```
+
+Then the lcm of the denominators of the exponents in `Supp(c_i)` is exactly
+`Lcal_i`. In particular every such exponent has denominator dividing `Lcal_i`.
+This is a statement about denominators, not the stronger and unnecessary claim
+that the only fractional exponents occurring are the recorded radii themselves.
+
+Proof: take a finite `K_N=k((t^{1/N}))` containing all relevant roots and radius
+monomials. Its Galois group over `k((t))` acts diagonally on exponents and fixes k.
+By the note after Def.5.1, p.179, the top disc is the minimal disc containing all
+roots of the stated polynomial product. That root set is Galois invariant, so its
+common truncation is fixed and belongs to `k((t))`. Thus the initial lattice is 1.
+In the normalized setting `c_s=0`: a root of the monic degree-n polynomial g
+cannot have order below -1, since then its y^n term would uniquely have smallest
+order, contradicting g=0.
+
+Inductively assume `c_r in K_L`, with L exactly the accumulated nonzero-radius
+lcm. The first display of Prop.5.3, p.180, defines the **prefix**
+`tau=c_r+C_r t^{delta_r}`. Set `L'=L` if `C_r=0`, otherwise
+`L'=lcm(L,den delta_r)`. The subgroup fixing `K_{L'}` fixes tau. It permutes the
+roots of the correct level-dependent polynomial
+`G_r=g product_{i=1}^r T_i^psi` and preserves the strict inequality
+`ord(xi-tau)>delta_r`. Hence it permutes exactly the packet in Prop.5.3's min
+display. That packet's minimal disc has radius `delta_{r-1}`; all packet roots
+have the same truncation below this radius. The subgroup therefore fixes that
+truncation `c_{r-1}`, proving `c_{r-1} in K_{L'}`.
+
+This also explains why a putative new denominator in the gap cannot be hidden:
+an automorphism fixing the prefix would move that common coefficient and produce
+two roots of the same packet parting before its printed minimal radius. Notice
+that the argument uses G_r at each step, not an unchanged product of all higher
+quasi-roots after they have left the packet.
+
+For equality, every selected nonzero term at `delta_j`, j>i, is an actual term
+of c_i: radii strictly increase down the tower and later truncation preserves
+earlier coefficients. Thus their denominator lcm divides the support lcm. The
+proved containment gives the opposite divisibility. This proves exactness and
+identifies the centre stabilizer as `Gal(Puiseux/K_{Lcal_i})`.
+
+**Zero-factor qualification.** `C_r=0` adds no term at `delta_r` and cannot
+enlarge the denominator lattice, including through any intervening a_j terms.
+It need not leave the centre itself unchanged. Intervening exponents on the
+already present lattice can occur. For example, the Galois-invariant root set
+`{epsilon t^{1/2}+epsilon t^{3/2} +/- t^2,
+epsilon t^{1/2}+t +/- t^2 : epsilon=+/-1}` is a polynomial packet model over
+`k((t))`. Its positive-slope disc has centre `t^{1/2}`, radius 1; choosing its
+zero coefficient at t gives centre `t^{1/2}+t^{3/2}`, radius 2. The denominator
+remains 2. This illustrates the gap issue, and is not asserted to be a Keller
+pair. Nor does “no lattice enlargement” mean “affinely removable”: a nonintegral
+exponent already on the old lattice can satisfy the former and fail the latter.
+The screen correctly keeps these as separate tests.
+
+**4. Necessity of the internal clauses, with printed locators.** At level j>=2,
+write
+`P=V_{j+1}d_j/d_{j+1}`, `Q=V_{j+1}(n-M_j)/d_{j+1}`,
+and `A=den(Lcal_j delta_j)`. Prop.4.6(1), p.170, gives
+`g_sigma=c p(pi)^{n/d_j}`, with `deg p=P`. The centre-fixing action just proved
+makes its root multiset invariant under a primitive A-th root of unity. Comparing
+the leading coefficient gives an eigenpolynomial; equivalently each nonzero root
+of p has an orbit of size exactly A, with constant multiplicity, while zero is fixed.
+
